GGEgg Stage
• The life cycle is completed in about 30 days (at a daily 
temperature of ~28°C) but may extend to 60-90 days in cooler 
temperatures;
• FAW does not diapause (a biological resting period); -infestations 
occur continuously throughout the year where pest is endemic;
• In non-endemic areas, migratory FAW arrive when 
environmental conditions allow and may have as few as one 
generation before they become locally extinct;
• The egg is dome shaped: the base is ﬂattened, curved upward to 
a broadly rounded point at the apex;
• The egg ~ 0.4 mm in diameter and 0.3 mm in height;
• Number of eggs per mass - 100 to 200;
• Total egg production per female, ~ 1,500, maximum 2,000;
• Duration of the egg stage is only 2-3 days during the warm 
months.

Larval Stage
▪ The FAW typically has six larval instars. Young larvae are 
greenish with a black head, the head turning a more orange 
color in the second instar;
▪ Larvae lengths of about 1 mm (instar 1) to 45 mm (instar 6); 
▪ In the second instar, but particularly the third instar, the dorsal 
surface of the body becomes brownish, and lateral white lines 
begin to form (Figure 1D);
▪ In the fourth to sixth instars the head is reddish brown, mottled 
with white, and the brownish body, bears white subdorsal and 
lateral lines;
▪ Elevated spots occur dorsally on the body; they are usually dark 
in color and bear spines.

Larval Stage
• The face of the mature larva may also be marked with a 
white inverted “Y” (Figure 1E);
• The best identifying feature of the FAW is a set of four 
large spots that form a square on the upper surface of 
the last segment of its body (Figure 1E);
• Larvae tend to conceal themselves during the brightest 
time of the day;
• Duration of larval stage ~ 14 days during the warm 
summer months and 30 days during cooler weather.

Pupal Stage
• The FAW normally pupates in the soil at a depth 2 to 8 cm.
• The larva constructs a loose cocoon by tying together 
particles of soil with silk. The cocoon is oval and 20 to 30 mm 
in length;
• The pupa is reddish brown in color measuring 14 to 18 mm in 
length and about 4.5 mm in width; 
• If the soil is too hard, larvae may web together leaf debris 
and other material to form a cocoon on the soil surface;
• Duration of the pupal stage - 8 to 9 days during the summer, 
- 20 to 30 days during cooler weather;
• The pupal stage cannot with stand protracted periods of cold 
weather.

Adult Stage
• Female moth - forewings are less distinctly marked, uniform greyish 
brown to a fine mottling of grey and brown;
• Male moth - the forewings shaded gray and brown, with triangular 
white spots at the tip and near the center of the wing;
• The hind wing is iridescent silver-white with a narrow dark border in 
both sexes;
• Adults are nocturnal, and are most active during warm, humid 
evenings;
• After a pre-oviposition period of 3 to 4 days, the female moth 
deposits most eggs during 4 to 5 days of life, some oviposition occurs 
for up to 3 weeks; 
• Duration of adult life is estimated to average about 10 days, with a 
range of about 7-21 days.

▪ The FAW has wide host range, > 80 
plants, prefers grasses;
▪ The frequently consumed crops - field 
maize and sweet maize, sorghum; 
▪ When the larvae are very numerous, 
they defoliate the preferred plants, 
acquire the typical “armyworm” habit, 
and disperse in large numbers, 
consuming nearly all vegetation in their 
path;
▪ Field crops injured - barley,, cotton, 
maize, millet, groundnut, rice, 
sorghum, soybean, sugarcane, tobacco, 
and wheat;

▪ Weeds hosts include:
➢ Bermuda grass, 
➢ Agrostis spp.; 
➢ Digitaria spp.;, 
➢ Sorghum halepense; 
➢ Ipomoea spp.; 
➢ nutsedge, 
➢ Cyperus spp.; 
➢ Amaranthus spp.
Host Range

▪ Early planting to avoid periods of high pest 
densities;
▪ Good soil preparation will affect pupae in 
the soil;
▪ Crop rotation with non-host plants and 
varietal choice;
▪ Sanitation- burn stubbles and cuttings after 
harvesting on infested fields. This will kill 
both the unhatched eggs, larvae, pupae and 
adults left on the field at harvest;
▪ In areas with high FAW infestation, disking 
or plowing can effectively reduce the 
survival rate of pupae in the soil;
▪ Early & regular visual inspection is one 
possibility to detect the pest’s presence;
▪ Plant early maturing varieties;
▪ Reduced tillage methods - increase of 
natural predators and parasitoids. 
Cultural Method

Safety Precautions
▪ Choose nationally registered, labelled insecticide, with a preference for those 
that are locally available, target specific, rapidly degrading and a low health risk; 
▪ Avoid fake, expired or banned products; 
▪ Rotate between pesticide groups with different modes of action to prevent 
resistance;
▪ A maximum of 2-3 sprays or more are recommended per season;
▪ Use proper personal protective equipment and follow the guidance provided on 
the pesticide label for rates of application, re-entry and preharvest intervals;
▪ Aim the nozzle at the plant whorl to ensure that the target is reached with the 
maximum spray volume.

Preventive Measures and Field Monitoring
• Preventive measures
✓Use high quality seed; 
✓Avoid late planting or staggered planting (plots of different 
ages);
✓Increase plant diversity in your plots;
• Field Monitoring:
✓Farmers should visit their fields frequently to observe, 
learn, and act;
